                <pb facs="0563" n="475" xml:id="pg475"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">837.01/24: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Panama</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Burdett</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Panama</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1933-10-06T11:00:00-05:00">October 6, 1933—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received 1:10 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>137. With reference to press reports Panaman recognition of present government
                    Cuba, acting President Diaz informs me that report is substantially correct and
                    that Panama has chosen to follow the principle of a continuous recognition by
                    instructing its diplomatic representative in Habana to establish conversations
                    with the government there.</p>
                <p>Acting President thought President Arias might call on Grau San Martín at
                    Habana.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Burdett</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
